# Scroll grammar

## Purpose

Tell the story for a reader who scrolls down a phone: one column, one panel
or two on screen at a time, with distance doing the work that the page turn
does in a book.

## Scope

Applies only when `comic_format` in `production/project.yaml` is `webtoon`.
Governs the `layout` block, the panel sizes, and the `page_turn` field of
every `page.yaml`, where a page is one scroll segment. Sits on top of
`rules/common/` and the domain layers, and narrows `layout/pacing.md` and
`layout/page-composition.md` for vertical reading without contradicting
them.

## Core Principles

- The screen is the frame. The reader never sees a whole segment, only the
  part of it that fits the phone.
- The column is one panel wide. Reading order is top to bottom and nothing
  else.
- Vertical space is time. The gap between two panels is a pause the reader
  feels with their thumb.
- Reveals are placed by distance. What must stay hidden sits further down
  than one screen from what sets it up.

## Segments

- A page (`PG_<issue>_###`) is one scroll segment: a tall image at the
  project's `page.width_px` and `page.height_px`. Segments stack in page
  order to form the episode.
- The reader does not see where one segment ends and the next begins. A
  segment's top and bottom edges are plain gutter in the episode's
  background color, so two segments join without a seam.
- A panel never spans two segments. Plan tall panels inside one segment, or
  give the tall image a segment of its own.
- `side` and `spread_with` are unused in webtoon projects and stay unset.
- `page_turn` is `none` or `continuation` on every segment except the last
  of an episode, which may be `cliffhanger`. Reveals inside an episode are
  built with distance (below), since no boundary is visible to hide them.
- Default density: 3 to 5 panels in a segment. The page budget in the series
  plan counts segments.

## The column

- Every panel takes the full width of the column or sits centered in it
  with the background on both sides. `layout.tiers` is a list of ones
  (`[1, 1, 1, 1]`), and `layout.reading_order` simply ascends.
- NEVER place two panels side by side when the reader must take them in
  order. On a phone they are too small to read, and the order is ambiguous.
  A single image may be divided by a vertical line for a simultaneous
  split (two callers, two places at once), and it is then one panel.
- Panel height carries emphasis, as area does on a paged layout. `small`
  panels are short strips, `large` panels fill most of a screen, and a
  `splash` is taller than the screen so the reader scrolls through it.
- Panels may drop their borders and float on the background. The background
  color between panels is part of the mood, and the color script sets it
  for each scene.
- Wide establishing views are hard to show in a narrow column. Use a tall
  view that moves from sky to ground, a sequence of `aspect` panels, or a
  full-width strip that accepts being small.

## Vertical rhythm and gutters as time

Gutter heights are stated as a share of the column width, so they hold at
any resolution. Defaults:

| Gap | Height | Reads as |
|---|---|---|
| Tight | 5 to 10% of column width | Rapid succession, the same instant |
| Normal | About 25% | The ordinary step between panels |
| Pause | 50 to 75% | A beat lands, and a breath follows |
| Break | 100 to 150% | Time passes, or the scene changes |

- A phone shows a height of roughly twice the column width. A gap longer
  than that leaves the screen empty, which is a strong effect to be used
  once or twice in an episode.
- Vary the rhythm. Equal gaps down the whole episode read as a list.
- A scene change uses a break gap, a change of background color, or both,
  and the arriving panel is anchored as `layout/panel-transitions.md`
  requires.
- Balloons and captions may sit in the gutter between panels. A caption
  alone in a long gap is the webtoon equivalent of a silent panel with a
  line of narration.
- Record intended gaps in `layout.notes` or in the panel `notes`, in the
  terms of the table, so the prompt and the review share them.

## Reveals by scroll distance

- Setup and reveal MUST NOT fit on one screen together. Put at least one
  screen height (twice the column width, by default) between the bottom of
  the setup panel and the top of the reveal.
- Fill that distance on purpose: a break gap, a darkening background, or a
  tall panel whose upper part gives nothing away.
- In a tall panel, the reader meets the top first. Compose it so the
  important element arrives last: the figure at the bottom of a long fall,
  the face at the end of a slow rise from the boots.
- Downward motion matches the scroll and feels fast. Upward motion works
  against it and feels like effort. Use both deliberately, in agreement
  with the scene's `geography`.
- End the episode on its `cliffhanger` panel, with space below it. Nothing
  follows the last panel except the credits the platform adds.

## Lettering in the scroll

- Balloons lead the eye downward. Alternate them left and right down a
  tall panel so the reader's eye crosses the art on its way.
- No element requires scrolling back up. A balloon is always at or below
  the top of the figure that speaks it and above the next balloon in the
  order.
- A balloon that would be cut by the screen edge at a normal reading
  position is moved or split. Keep each balloon shorter than a third of a
  screen.
- Sizes and word budgets for phone reading are in `episode-deliverables.md`.

## Paged techniques out of place

- No page turns, left and right pages, or spreads. Ignore
  `rules/paged/` entirely.
- No multi-column grids. A paged page shrunk to the width of a phone is
  unreadable, so adapting a paged comic means re-planning every panel for
  the column.
- No print geometry. Trim, bleed, and spine margins do not apply. The only
  margins are the ones the phone and the platform impose.

## Validation

- `schemas/page.schema.json` checks the layout fields, and
  `scripts/check-continuity.js` checks that `layout.tiers` sums to the panel
  count and that `layout.reading_order` is a permutation of the panels'
  `order` values.
- A webtoon page whose `layout.tiers` contains a value above 1, or whose
  `side` or `spread_with` is set, is visible in the file. The layout-artist
  rejects it in the self-review of `/page-plan`. No script reads
  `comic_format` to enforce it today.
- Gutter heights and reveal distances exist only in notes and in pixels.
  They are checked on the thumbnails and on the rendered segments, under
  the `page_structure` check of the visual review.
- Whether a reveal survives depends on what one screen shows. Review the
  stacked segments at phone width by scrolling them, in the `web` export or
  on a device. Looking at a segment as a whole image hides the problem.
